he waypoint yaw/pitch/roll rotation equals each frame's own attitude quaternion (max matrix element difference 6.9e-07). Waypoint = end-of-drive localization; its own accuracy is not stated by NASA in the file and is assumed to be metres to tens of metres.","Frames: all Navcam-left frames of this site+drive that pass the fixed usability rule; of those a deterministic greedy subset (boresight azimuths >= 40 deg apart, starting at the seed frame, clockwise) is downloaded to stay inside the download budget. 36 frames passed the rule, 5 used. Seed/'single frame' = the frame of the legacy single-frame test.","Skyline traced per frame with the SAME settings as the single-frame tests (sigma 1.5 px, threshold 4.0, 5-px median smoothing, >12 px running-median outlier rejection); not tuned. Each frame's columns are converted to true azimuth/elevation with its own CAHVORE model and the waypoint attitude, binned to 2-deg cells (>= 10 traced columns, median elevation); frames are merged per cell by median. Cells with no frame or no accepted trace are NaN (null) and are not scored.","Rover-hardware rule: no azimuth sector is masked. On the development frames the rover deck/RTG appears only in the lowest part of the frame, well below the skyline, so the geometric rule is: nothing masked. Not examined for held-out frames before the run.","Matching: heading known to +-10 deg (11 shifts of 2 deg); one elevation offset per candidate (clipped to +-0.5 deg); terrain closer than 150 m is not used to form the predicted horizon (CTX samples >= 150 m and MOLA only) but every traced azimuth is scored for every candidate (v2; v1 excluded such azimuths per candidate, see README 8b); score = RMS over all valid azimuths. The same near-field and offset rules are used in all four ablation rows; only the frame set and the backdrop change. A candidate must score at least 20 azimuths AND 50% of the valid azimuths: this guard was added after the first development run, in which the single-frame best candidate scored only 20 of 48 azimuths because the near-field rule dropped the rest (a degenerate 'win'). It was not tuned on position error.","Horizon = max over CTX samples (40 m, to 15 km) and MOLA samples (250 m, to 120 km, only outside the CTX footprint or beyond 15 km), Mars curvature term, eye 2 m above the CTX elevation at the candidate. CTX minus MOLA at 400 random points inside the DTM: mean -3.6 m, std 24.9 m, so no vertical offset is applied. MOLA is 463 m/pixel and median-binned: it cannot represent terrain finer than that.","CTX part of horizons_ext is checked equal to skyline.horizons (imported, unchanged) at 24 candidates every run (max difference < 1e-6 deg).","A 20 m DTM cannot contain metre-scale rocks in the foreground; those rows of the skyline are part of the photo but not of the model.","Downloads this run: 0 images (cached ones not counted)."],"method":"Real Navcam frames (NASA/JPL-Caltech raw images), skyline traced and matched against CTX DTM + MOLA horizon; not simulated.","verdict":"FAILED (no better than the prior) (rule fixed before the held-out runs: FIX = error <= 500 m and uncertainty <= 1000 m; WEAK = error below the prior offset). Full method (panorama of 5 frames, 100% of azimuths scored, CTX + MOLA backdrop): error 2770 m, uncertainty radius 2280 m, min RMS 0.980 deg (prior offset 1757 m). Ablation errors (m): single/CTX 4082, single+MOLA 494, panorama/CTX 3146, full 2770. At the waypoint itself the full-method RMS is 1.810 deg (residual median +0.08, 5-95% range -1.73 to +5.06 deg). One position, one run: not a statistical result."}